        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<P> This was the first flight of this experiment, in which the hypothesis that starch concentration in plant tissue decreases due to the space/microgravity environment was tested and possible mechanistic causes for the changes in starch concentration were investigated. Measurements were made of starch and soluble sugar concentrations, critical biosynthetic and degradative enzyme activities, localization of the starch grains and the plastids, structural and ultra structural make-up of different tissues within soybean (Glycine max) seedlings, and detailed measurements of growth and biomass partitioning.     <P>APPROACH:<P> This experiment flew on STS-68 in September 1994. This was an 11-day mission on which two canisters filled with twenty-six soybeans (Glycine max) each were flown. The soybeans were harvested after landing.  Postflight measurements were made of growth, biomass partitioning, gas concentrations in the canisters, carbohydrate concentrations and related enzyme activity in the cotyledons and ultrastructural aspects of cotyledon, hypocotyl and root tissue sections.    <P>RESULTS:<P> The hypothesis that starch concentration would be reduced in the space-grown cotyledons was supported by the results of this experiment. Starch concentration in the cotyledons was reduced by approximately 25% in the space tissue compared to the ground controls.  Measurements of 11 different enzyme activities related to starch and sugar metabolism were conducted. Only adenosine diphosphate (ADP) glucose pyrophosphorylase, a rate-limiting enzyme in starch synthesis, was affected by the space-flight environment. The activity of this enzyme was lower in the space-grown cotyledons than in the ground controls, suggesting that the lower starch concentration seen was due to a lower activity of this enzyme. <P>
        </div>

